;;; A range in a rule's `:match` matches nothing, in silence — M1e2 **W20**,
;;; register row `W20/range-in-match` (`corpus/wrong_answers.toml`), banked
;;; by S1e2.2.20 (2026-11-05) from S1e2.2.18's parked note, ruled by the user
;;; on 2026-11-04 (Q-M1e2.13 (a)).
;;;
;;; ── What this program is ───────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; One fact, `(n 2)`, and one rule whose pattern writes the range `1..3`
;;; where a fact's argument goes.
;;;
;;; ── The answer, by hand ────────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; A range is cardinality metadata (`01_grammar.md`'s `RANGE` row, UML-style
;;; `1..*`), and no fact can hold one: the user ruled that a range in a rule
;;; is refused at load, with a line in Ein's spelling naming the rule and the
;;; slot. Every command that loads the program exits 1 (`ein test`: 2).
;;;
;;; ── What the engine answered, and why it does not any more ─────────────
;;;
;;; Until S1e2.2.20 the program loaded, and `r` never fired: the rule
;;; compiler lowered a range as an opaque slot, which unifies with nothing
;;; (`compile.rs`' `slot`). Root held no `hit`, H was empty, and R was `{∅}`,
;;; exit 0, with no word of the range (`ein solve FILE` at `c73a0796`,
;;; release and debug; every mode and `ein oracle` the same). Since then the
;;; loader refuses it: *kb load error: rule 'r': its :match writes 1..3 as an
;;; argument of n, and no fact can hold it — a range is cardinality metadata,
;;; and a rule matches and concludes facts*.

(relation n Node) (relation hit Node)
(n 2)
(rule r () :match (n 1..3) :assert (hit r))
